# New-requirement intake

<!-- canonical:intake -->
**By default the agent continues every operator request in the current chat, worktree and branch; it spins up a new worktree only when the operator explicitly asks for one — by typing `/jarvis-new-task` or saying "new task" / "new worktree" / "separate PR".**

## Default: same workspace

- Treat a mid-session request as part of the current work, even when it looks like a new topic.
  Do not redirect the operator to `/jarvis-new-task`, do not refuse to implement, and do not
  stop to ask whether it should be split out.
- If the request needs code that lives in **another** worktree (e.g. the page the operator is
  looking at is served from a sibling worktree), make the change where that code lives rather
  than declining or re-creating it — and say which worktree you edited.
- Mention at most once, in the closing summary, if the extra scope will make the PR noticeably
  harder to review; the operator decides whether to split.

## Explicit opt-in: `/jarvis-new-task`

Only when the operator types `/jarvis-new-task <requirement>` (or explicitly asks for a new
task / worktree / separate PR) does the agent run:

```bash
python3 scripts/new_requirement.py --requirement "<operator's text>"
```

The script:
1. Creates a sibling git worktree on a fresh branch off `origin/main`
2. Runs `start_pr_session.py` (cost session + brief + launcher)
3. Opens Cursor in a new window with a jam-mode deeplink
4. Creates a GitHub tracking issue and seeds the phase cache in both repos

**After `/jarvis-new-task` fires, stop.** No clarifying questions before running — refinement
happens in the jam gate of the new chat space.

## Why this rule exists

Assuming every new topic needs its own worktree bounced the operator out of their flow and
blocked small follow-ups (2026-10-03, Issue #337: a table-polish request was redirected to
`/jarvis-new-task` instead of being done). Worktree isolation is still the right tool for
genuinely separate work — the operator opts into it explicitly.

